## 7. What I Actually Did in the Project

### Stage 1 — Data Understanding

I profiled the dataset to understand structure, data types, missing values, cardinality, duplicates, date coverage, business-rule consistency, revenue/profit relationships, and shipping/delivery fields.

The dataset contains **180,519 records and 53 original columns**.

### Stage 2 — Data Cleaning & Quality Validation

I prepared the analytical dataset by removing columns with no analytical value, handling missing values, standardizing data types and categorical fields, converting dates, validating identifiers, validating sales consistency, validating shipping-day logic, validating delivery-risk consistency, and identifying profit outliers.

An important analytical decision was to flag extreme profit values rather than automatically delete them because unusual profit can represent legitimate business events.

### Stage 3 — Feature Engineering

I engineered features for time, delivery, sales, profitability, customers, products, shipping, regions, risk, and customer behavior, including order revenue, AOV, delay, late flag, shipping efficiency, profit margin, loss-order indicators, customer value segments, product rankings, demand categories, high-risk orders, and discount indicators.

## 8. Exploratory & Business Analysis

The EDA was organized around business questions rather than only statistical plots.

Major areas:

- Revenue analytics: total revenue, yearly growth, monthly trends, revenue contribution, market performance, product concentration.
- Profitability analytics: total profit, margin, market/product profit, loss-making products, discount versus profitability.
- Customer analytics: customer value, segments, top customers, frequency, average basket value.
- Operational analytics: late-delivery rate, shipping-mode performance, regional delivery risk, actual versus scheduled shipping time, shipping efficiency.

